* SDWBS Conventions

** Status Markers

   Norg provides a full set of TODO status markers. The SDWBS uses all
   of them to express different element states:

   - `( )` — /Undone/: not started; no children have started
   - `(x)` — /Done/: complete; all children are `(x)`, or leaf node is done
   - `(-)` — /In progress/: at least one child is `(x)` and at least
     one is `( )` or `(-)`
   - `(=)` — /On hold/: work paused due to external blocker or reprioritisation
   - `(_)` — /Cancelled/: scope cut, no longer planned
   - `(?)` — /Uncertain/: needs further input, clarification, or decision
   - `(!)` — /Urgent/: high-priority item requiring immediate attention

   Status propagates upward automatically. A parent's status is always
   derived from its children — never set independently.

   Examples:
   - If Design `(x)`, Implementation `(x)`, Verification `( )`,
     Validation `( )` → parent stage is `(-)`
   - If all four phases are `(x)` → parent stage is `(x)`
   - If none have started → parent stage is `( )`
   - If Backend `(x)`, Frontend `(-)`, Mobile `( )` →
     Implementation is `(-)`
   - If a feature is descoped → mark it `(_)` cancelled; parent
     recalculates from remaining active children


** Element Type Tags

   Every WBS element carries a type tag in square brackets:

   - `[APP]` — Top-level application or system boundary
   - `[feature]` — User-facing feature module (follows D/I/V/V lifecycle)
   - `[infra]` — Infrastructure component (flat, verified by CI tooling)
   - `[ops]` — Operational concern (CI/CD, monitoring, alerting)
   - `[lib]` — Shared library or utility (consumed by features)
   - `[ext]` — External dependency (design handoffs, legal, third-party)

   Tags are placed after the WBS number and before the element name:
   `** (-) 42.2. [feature] Chat {* 42.2}`

   Infrastructure and ops elements use flat structures — they do not
   follow the four-phase lifecycle. Shared libraries track their own
   stages when they have independent release cycles.


** Section Patterns — Feature Lifecycle

   Feature modules (`[feature]`) decompose each stage into four phases:

   ~ *Design* — architecture, user stories, data models, flow diagrams
   ~ *Implementation* — source code, split by platform sub-domain
   ~ *Verification* — unit tests, integration tests, E2E tests
   ~ *Validation* — QA scenarios, acceptance scripts, sign-off

   /Individual defects are tracked in the issue tracker; the SDWBS
   records aggregated counts in the Implementation phase./


** Implementation Sub-domains

   Implementation splits into platform sub-domains at one heading level
   deeper than the phase:

   - *Backend* (BE) — API endpoints, services, data access, business logic
   - *Frontend* (FE) — Web UI components, state management, API integration
   - *Mobile* — Native/cross-platform screens, device APIs, offline support

   Not every feature requires all platforms. Omit sub-domains that don't
   apply (e.g. a backend-only migration has no FE or Mobile section).

   Verification and Validation remain unified — they reference
   platform-specific test suites in bullets but do not split into
   sub-headings.

   Example:
   @code norg
   **** (x) 42.1.1.2. Implementation {* 42.1.1.2}
   ***** (x) 42.1.1.2.1. Backend {* 42.1.1.2.1}
         - (x) User registration endpoint with email verification
         - (x) Password hashing service
   ***** (x) 42.1.1.2.2. Frontend {* 42.1.1.2.2}
         - (x) Registration form with real-time validation
   ***** (x) 42.1.1.2.3. Mobile {* 42.1.1.2.3}
         - (x) Registration screen with biometric option
   @end


** Task Items & Acceptance Criteria

   Tasks are status-marked bullets under each platform sub-domain.
   Acceptance criteria (AC) are optional nested sub-bullets that define
   "done" for each task:

   @code norg
   - (x) User registration endpoint with email verification
     - AC: returns 201 + sends verification email within 5s
     - AC: 409 on duplicate email with descriptive error
     - AC: rate-limited to 3 requests/min per IP
   @end

   Guidelines:
   - 3–6 tasks per platform sub-domain per stage
   - AC lines on tasks with non-obvious completion criteria
   - Simple tasks can omit AC (single descriptive line is enough)
   - Bug fixes may be aggregated: "Bug fixes (N items)"


** Stage Naming

   - `Stage 1` through `Stage N` — planned and committed release scopes
   - `Future` — aspirational items not yet scheduled for any stage

   Stages are not strict waterfall gates. Individual modules may complete
   their stage work independently — infrastructure often leads features.

   Not every module requires work in every stage. When a module has no
   deliverables for a given stage, the stage number is simply skipped
   (e.g. a module may have Stage 1 and Stage 3 with no Stage 2 entry).


** Numbering Context

   A project's WBS number can start at any value (e.g. `42.` instead of
   `1.`). This supports embedding the SDWBS within a larger organisational
   WBS where the project is one node in a programme-level tree.


** Companion Files

   When a module spans multiple stages with significant detail, its
   content moves to a companion file named after its WBS number
   (e.g. `42.1. Authentication.norg`). The parent file retains a summary
   with a cross-reference.

   Criteria for splitting:
   - Module has 2+ stages with non-trivial Implementation detail
   - Inline expansion would exceed ~80 lines in the parent file
   - Module is frequently updated independently


** Cross-references

   WBS elements use anchors `{* 42.1.2}` for dependency tracking.
   Pre-requisites list forward dependencies (what this element needs).
   Reverse dependencies (what depends on this element) are navigable
   via editor tooling (e.g. norg backlink navigation).


** Ownership

   Each feature or module may include an `Owner:` field in its
   description (e.g. `Owner: @backend-team`). This identifies the
   responsible team but does not replace the ticket tracker for
   individual assignment.

   The SDWBS tracks *work scope*, not people. Sprint assignment,
   individual task ownership, and capacity planning live in the
   ticket tracker.

   Per-owner filtered views (e.g. `@backend-team.norg`) can be
   generated to show each team's slice of the WBS tree.


** Extra Fields

   Nodes may carry additional metadata fields as plain-text lines
   in their description:

   - `Owner:` — responsible team or individual (e.g. `Owner: @backend-team`)
   - `Effort:` — T-shirt size estimate (e.g. `Effort: M (2-3 sprints)`)
   - `Outcome:` — measurable success criteria, placed in the Validation
     phase (e.g. `Outcome: signup conversion >60%`)

   These fields are informational and do not affect status propagation.
   They exist to provide context for project planning and reporting.


** External Dependencies

   External blockers (design handoffs, legal reviews, third-party
   approvals) are tracked as their own WBS subtree using the `[ext]`
   element type tag. Features reference external dependency nodes in
   their pre-requisites — when the external node reaches `(x)`, the
   dependent feature can proceed.

   Example:
   @code norg
   ** (-) 42.11. [ext] External Dependencies {* 42.11}
   *** (x) 42.11.1. Design Handoff — Authentication {* 42.11.1}
   *** (-) 42.11.3. Design Handoff — Notes {* 42.11.3}
   @end

   Referenced from a feature:
   @code norg
   Pre-requisites:
   - (-) {* 42.11.3} Design Handoff — Notes Stage 3 specs
   @end


** Filtered Views

   The SDWBS supports multiple /view files/ — projections of the same
   data filtered by different criteria. These are separate `.norg` files
   that reference the main WBS via anchors (`{* X.Y}`):

   - `critical-path.norg` — only blocking chains and their downstream impact
   - `@team-name.norg` — filtered tree showing one team's owned nodes
   - Per-stage views, per-status views, etc.

   View files are derived artifacts. They can be manually maintained or
   script-generated from the canonical WBS files. They link back to
   source nodes and do not duplicate content.


** Scaling

   The SDWBS scales by splitting nodes into companion files. Any node
   can be extracted into its own file when it grows beyond ~80 lines.
   Companion files can themselves reference further companion files,
   creating an arbitrarily deep file tree while keeping each individual
   file navigable.

   For large projects (20+ features), consider grouping features under
   sub-apps or domains at level 2 (e.g. `42.A. [domain] Collaboration`
   containing Chat, Notes, and Sharing).
